// The paper, and the order a power-on happens in.
//
// This is the RISC-V board's stage, and the shape is the ARM board's: one
// sprite holds the processor and everything it can reach, one holds the
// display, and the stage's only job is to start them in the right order and
// get out of the way.
//
// There is one drawing sprite and one picture. The **Monitor** draws the
// graphics card's scanout, and the card's scanout is everything this machine
// puts on the Stage: the Linux console is pixels the kernel's `fbcon` wrote
// through `simplefb` into the card's own memory, and Doom's picture is pixels
// its video driver wrote there directly. Nothing in this project draws text
// out of a font table -- there is no font table in it to draw from.
//
// The order is the ARM stage's and it is a real power-on order:
//
//   1. The machine resets the bus and the hart and lays the guest into RAM.
//   2. The monitor starts drawing, before the hart runs, so a guest that
//      paints its first frame early is not painting into a monitor that has
//      not started.
//   3. Only then does the hart run, and the script that runs it does not
//      return until the guest stops.

stage {
    costume "paper" = "assets/backdrop.svg";

    broadcast "Machine.powerOn";
    broadcast "Machine.run";
    broadcast "Monitor.run";

    on flag_clicked {
        events::broadcast_and_wait("Machine.powerOn");
        events::broadcast("Monitor.run");
        events::broadcast("Machine.run");
    }
}
